{"archetypes":[{"name":"state-machine-transitions","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan rewires run_bump_phase return-code routing and adds a new internal helper; every non-success exit path must be traced to the correct next state.","prompt_body":"For each return-code branch of the new `run_step8b_rebase_rebump_internal` function described in the plan, verify the plan specifies how `run_bump_phase` handles that result — including partial-rebase partial-state, `repo_unavailable=true` bail (return 2), forked bail, and hard-failure `exit_stall 8b`. Check whether the plan's `advance_phase bump` + loop continuation after internal success could re-enter the bump classify path on a HEAD that already has a bump commit, and whether the classify-NONE idempotency guarantee is explicitly preserved in the plan's approach section. Verify that `STALL_TRACKING=false` and `CALLER_KIND=ship_pr_pre_push` are only emitted on the non-bump-only conflict branch, not on hard failures that should route to `exit_stall`. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"token-synchrony","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan introduces ship-pr-rrr-phase14-postbump as a new RESUME_PHASE token that must be symmetrically produced and consumed across ship-pr.sh, conflict-resolution.md, and the resume dispatch arm.","prompt_body":"Verify that the plan names every location where `ship-pr-rrr-phase14-postbump` is produced (state_set_many writes) and every location where it is consumed (resume dispatch arm in ship-pr.sh, Phase 4 exit-0 handler in conflict-resolution.md), and that the plan leaves no consumer reading a token that a producer no longer emits. Check whether the plan addresses in-flight state files from runs that started before this change and may contain the old `step8b_rebase` or `step8_apply_bump_same_version` tokens in a persisted `CALLER_KIND` or `RESUME_PHASE` key — if ship-pr.sh's resume dispatch no longer has an arm for those tokens, a user resuming a stalled run mid-upgrade would hit `die_usage` or a dead-arm. Also confirm the plan's retirement of the `step8_apply_bump_same_version` exit-5 arm is safe given that `_run_step8_same_version_mechanically()` is described as already internal. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"harness-coverage","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan names three test harnesses with high-level assertions; the new resume route requires multi-step fixture setup that the harness structure may not currently support.","prompt_body":"For each failure mode listed in the plan's Failure Modes section, verify that a specific test assertion in one of the named harnesses (`test-ship-pr.sh`, `test-ship-pr-rebase-phase14.sh`, `test-implement-rebase-macro.sh`) is also listed in the plan's Testing Strategy or Files section; flag failure modes that have no corresponding test entry. Check whether the plan's `test-ship-pr-rebase-phase14.sh` update requires the harness to first exercise the new internal step8b path (to populate the state file with `RESUME_PHASE=ship-pr-rrr-phase14-postbump`) before testing Phase 4 resume — and whether the plan describes how that fixture state is established. Verify the plan mentions running `test-step2-dispatch.sh` only to confirm no stale `step8b_rebase` exit-5 expectation remains, and that this harness is not expected to exercise the new internal path directly. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
